<section>
    <h2>Say what, hide how</h2>
    <p>
        Tests that click by selector everywhere get long, and when the page changes, every one of them breaks. A
        <strong>page object</strong> is a class that knows how a page (or one part of it) works: it holds the
        locators and offers actions. Tests say what they do; the page object knows how.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>A page object</h2>
<pre><code>public class SignInPage(IPage page)
{
    private ILocator Email =&gt; page.GetByLabel("Email");
    private ILocator Password =&gt; page.GetByLabel("Password");
    private ILocator SignIn =&gt; page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Sign in" });

    public Task OpenAsync() =&gt; page.GotoAsync("/sign-in");

    public async Task SignInAsync(string email, string password)
    {
        await Email.FillAsync(email);
        await Password.FillAsync(password);
        await SignIn.ClickAsync();
    }
}

// in a test
var signIn = new SignInPage(Page);
await signIn.OpenAsync();
await signIn.SignInAsync("crawler@example.com", password);
await Expect(Page.GetByText("Welcome back")).ToBeVisibleAsync();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Components</h2>
    <p>
        When a page repeats the same shape (product cards, table rows, panels), model one of them, rooted at a
        locator. Everything inside starts from the root, so it can't reach into its neighbor:
    </p>
<pre><code>public class ProductCard(ILocator root)
{
    public ILocator Price =&gt; root.GetByTestId("price");
    public Task AddToBasketAsync() =&gt; root.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Add" }).ClickAsync();
}

var lantern = new ProductCard(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Article, new() { Name = "Lantern" }));
await lantern.AddToBasketAsync();</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Keep most assertions in the test.</strong> Page objects find things and do things; the test decides
        what's right. That way two tests can use the same page object and expect different outcomes.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Drafting Table</h2>
    <p>
        Four blank blueprints, shuffled, and a work order. Fill every blueprint as the work order says and stamp it.
        One component object and a loop will do it. Ten seconds.
    </p>
</section>
